<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- W3C SCXML 6.2 `<send>` `<param>` payload delivery on the two paths that
     had no runtime witness.

     Both were fixed at the template layer and both stayed unproven, for the
     same reason: no committed fixture had a machine of the required shape, so
     the suites could only show that nothing regressed. The absence is also
     why the defects survived — a shape nobody generates is a shape nobody
     breaks and nobody fixes.

     Two independent paths, deliberately reaching different final states so a
     failure names which one broke rather than reporting "payload lost":

       child -> parent, from a machine with no script engine
         The child needs no script engine at all: every one of its params
         is a literal, so each folds at codegen time. Kotlin
         used to gate param emission on the *machine* needing one, so this
         shape shipped a `<send>` with its params dropped. The right question
         is whether *this send* needs an engine, which is what the C++
         template had always asked. Reaching `passChild` means the payload
         crossed the invoke boundary from an engine-less child.

       #_internal, from a machine that does have an engine
         Sends to `#_internal` used to drop their payload unconditionally —
         the internal-raise path took no event data, so params were built and
         then discarded. Reaching `pass` means an internal raise now carries
         its params to the receiving transition's `_event.data`.

     The parent has to *read* the payload to judge it, so it needs an
     engine; the child only emits literals, so it needs none, and being
     engine-less is precisely the property under test. The two cannot be
     one machine.

     Both declare `datamodel="ecmascript"`. The child read `null` for as
     long as the attribute reached no decision, and that reading was
     wrong twice over: it is not what makes the child engine-less (its
     literal params are), and W3C SCXML B.1.4 gives the Null data model
     no value expression language for the `expr` attributes it carries.

     Values are strings on the first two paths: `<param>` payload marshalling
     is not the axis there, and a numeric literal would additionally assert how
     each backend maps number types across the boundary.

     A third phase asks exactly that, because nothing else did. Two claims,
     both about what a `<param>` VALUE is when it arrives:

       a number stays a number
         `expr="7"` is the Number 7, and `_event.data.n === 7` is how a
         document reads it. A backend that stringifies on the way through
         delivers "7", which `===` finds unequal — a guard that silently takes
         the other branch, which is the failure mode this axis keeps producing.

       two params of one name keep both values AND their type
         W3C SCXML 6.2 lets a name repeat, and test178 in the IRP corpus is
         MANUAL: the tester reads the log to confirm both pairs are there, so
         no automated channel has ever checked it. C++ folds duplicates into an
         array — measured 2026-08-16 in `EventDataHelper::mergeParams`, whose
         `values.size() > 1` branch publishes the STRING vector and drops the
         typed values it is holding at the same moment. The claim is the array
         AND its element types: `d.length === 2 && d[0] === 1`.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0" datamodel="ecmascript" initial="awaitChild"
       name="send_param_payload">

    <state id="awaitChild">
        <invoke type="scxml" id="inv_emitter">
            <content>
                <scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
                       version="1.0" datamodel="ecmascript" initial="emit">
                    <state id="emit">
                        <onentry>
                            <send target="#_parent" event="fromChild">
                                <param name="value" expr="'42'"/>
                            </send>
                        </onentry>
                        <transition target="sent"/>
                    </state>
                    <final id="sent"/>
                </scxml>
            </content>
        </invoke>

        <transition event="fromChild"
                    cond="_event.data &amp;&amp; _event.data.value === '42'"
                    target="internalPhase"/>
        <!-- Same event, no cond: the payload did not arrive intact. -->
        <transition event="fromChild" target="failChildPayload"/>
    </state>

    <state id="internalPhase">
        <onentry>
            <send target="#_internal" event="loopback">
                <param name="carried" expr="'kept'"/>
            </send>
        </onentry>
        <transition event="loopback"
                    cond="_event.data &amp;&amp; _event.data.carried === 'kept'"
                    target="typedPhase"/>
        <transition event="loopback" target="failInternalPayload"/>
    </state>

    <!-- W3C SCXML 6.2: what a `<param>` value IS on arrival. The two claims
         are separated into two transitions so a failure names which one broke:
         a number that arrived stringified is a different defect from a
         duplicate name that lost one of its values. -->
    <state id="typedPhase">
        <onentry>
            <send target="#_internal" event="typed">
                <param name="n" expr="7"/>
                <param name="d" expr="1"/>
                <param name="d" expr="2"/>
            </send>
        </onentry>
        <transition event="typed" cond="_event.data.n !== 7" target="failNumberType"/>
        <transition event="typed"
                    cond="_event.data.d.length === 2 &amp;&amp; _event.data.d[0] === 1 &amp;&amp; _event.data.d[1] === 2"
                    target="pass"/>
        <transition event="typed" target="failDuplicateParams"/>
    </state>

    <final id="pass"/>
    <final id="failChildPayload"/>
    <final id="failInternalPayload"/>
    <final id="failNumberType"/>
    <final id="failDuplicateParams"/>
</scxml>
